# The Crystal Ball as a Fire Hazard

The clerk's warning wasn't about spirits at all — it was about **fire**.

A crystal ball acts as a **lens**. When sunlight passes through a spherical glass object, it can focus the light into a single concentrated point — the same way a magnifying glass can start a fire. If the woman left the crystal ball sitting near a window in an empty house, focused sunlight could ignite curtains, furniture, or other flammable materials, potentially burning the house down.

So "cover it when you leave your house" was practical fire-safety advice, and the woman's assumption about spirits was a humorous misdirection — she thought the warning was supernatural when it was actually scientific. [end of text]


